    "blurb": "The show bursts with a scorching Good Lovin’ jam, a soaring Dark Star, and Duane Allman's fiery solos on It Hurts Me Too and Sugar Magnolia. Pigpen’s harmonica shines throughout, and the band maintains relentless momentum from start to finish.",
    "review": "April 26, 1971 at the Fillmore East finds the Dead in top form, delivering a set that crackles with electricity from the opening Bertha through a blistering second half. Highlights include a smoking Good Lovin’ jam that morphs into a funky drum break, an expansive Dark Star that rides seamlessly into Wharf Rat, and Duane Allman's guest solos that elevate It Hurts Me Too and Sugar Magnolia to legendary status. Pigpen’s gritty harmonica and vocal contributions add extra bite to the blues numbers, while Phil’s thunderous bass and Billy’s propulsive drumming keep the momentum locked in. The performance is captured beautifully on several soundboard recordings, but the Betty‑board source stands out for its crystal‑clear fidelity, making this a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
